# WHERE WORK COMMITS IN THIS REPO, and it is a family rather than one name:
# every session is given its own `claude/*` branch, so no single branch name
# could be written here. That is why this file did not exist until now — and
# its absence failed the shared gates workflow at step one, which meant every
# gate after it never ran on a runner at all.
#
# The flow this states: commit on the session branch, push that same history to
# `staging` for the on-device pass, merge to `main` by pull request. Nothing
# commits on `staging` (it is pushed to), and `main` is reached by a rebase
# merge on GitHub, which no local hook sees — JPS_PROMOTE is the door for the
# rare local commit there, said out loud.
work=claude/*
promote=main
escape=JPS_PROMOTE

# Run on every commit: the quick look keeps rendered previews on the device and
# hands them back instead of decoding again, so a change to the code a preview
# is MADE of has to move the declared version or the app serves a picture this
# build would not make. A comment asking for that was written first; this is the
# version that can refuse.
also=tools/preview-version-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: the install prompt's pictures of the app. Every field in
# a manifest `screenshots` entry is a claim about a file — that it is there,
# that it is the type and the SIZE it says — and a wrong one is refused in
# silence, leaving the plain prompt that looks exactly like having no
# screenshots at all. This reads the bytes and compares.
also=tools/manifest-shots-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: the hub's FAST surface gates — placeholders and examples,
# set-apart quotations, and the no-tables rule over tracked markdown. They ran
# in CI and nowhere else, so a placeholder added to the Help filter went red on
# the runner three pushes after the commit that introduced it, and nothing
# local could refuse it. The two slow ones (privacy, third person: 10s and 19s)
# stay in CI on purpose — half a minute per commit gets routed around.
also=tools/hub-surface-gates.mjs

# Run on every commit: the ⓘ dialog's Roadmap is parsed out of NOTES.md by
# vite.config.ts, and that parser is wrapped in a try/catch returning an EMPTY
# LIST — so every way of getting it wrong builds cleanly, deploys green, and
# shows the reader a dialog with nothing in it. A session appending a section
# anchored on the string "## Shipped (roadmap archive)", which is MENTIONED in
# quotes inside the roadmap section's own blockquote 800 lines above the real
# heading; the section landed inside the roadmap, its `## ` stopped the parser
# before the first bullet, and the in-app Roadmap went from 18 items to 0 across
# five commits, to staging and to production, with nothing anywhere going red.
also=tools/notes-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: the hub commit is written in two files — `.doctrine-sync`
# (what this repo has RECONCILED with) and the `uses: .../hub-gates.yml@<sha>`
# pin (what CI RUNS the shared gates from). They are the same fact and they
# drift silently, because adopting is one command and editing a workflow is
# another and nothing about the tree looks different afterwards. What it costs:
# CI keeps running the gates from an older commit, every one goes green, and a
# rule added to the hub in between is enforced nowhere while the marker says it
# has been read. This repo had no marker at all and a pin four weeks behind.
also=tools/hub-pin-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: a bare `var(--x)` naming a property nothing defines. CSS
# has no error for that — the declaration is dropped at computed-value time and
# an inherited property just INHERITS, so the element renders a plausible colour
# and every sweep that reads the rendered DOM agrees with it. The crop bar
# declared `--txt-1` in three places; no stylesheet in this repo has ever
# defined that name. It took reverse-mapping rendered colours back to tokens for
# the palette spec to notice, which is a browser run — this is a text scan that
# refuses the commit.
also=tools/token-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: palettes/studio.json is GENERATED from public/palette.css
# by a browser, so it is committed like the branch guard's own hook — and like
# every committed artefact it can go stale in the tree while CI stays green,
# measuring a palette nobody sees. Regenerating needs a browser and a served
# build, which no hook has; noticing that the source moved and the artefact did
# not needs neither. The other half of that artefact, the `_renders` list, is
# measured from the whole app and is re-measured by section 4 of the a11y walk.
also=tools/palette-spec-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: the lens profile store's two doors. A brightness curve is
# DERIVED on save and JUDGED on read, and nothing held the two to each other —
# so a payload whose falloff was not NBINS long saved cleanly and was refused on
# every read afterwards, wholesale, taking the colour curves with it. A measured
# lens quietly stopped correcting anything and the app said nothing. Both
# functions are pure, so this needs no browser and costs milliseconds.
also=tools/lens-store-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: every exported function says what it takes, what it does
# and what it gives back. The repo commented richly and stated contracts almost
# nowhere — 10 of 246 when this was added — and the gap cost a release: bumpFrom
# never said its result has to pass bumpProblem, so tightening bumpProblem broke
# saving and loading against each other and a measured lens stopped working with
# nothing said. The backlog is declared in .contract-allow and can only shrink.
also=tools/contract-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: the module map in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md is GENERATED from
# the comment each source file opens with, and held identical to it. A reference
# that goes stale is worse than none, because it is consulted — that document was
# a day old and still named a look that had been renamed, and nothing could see
# it. Generating it means a new module cannot be added without appearing there, a
# deleted one cannot linger, and a purpose that changes in the code changes in
# the map. It also refuses a source file that does not say what it is.
also=tools/architecture-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: every open roadmap item carries a decision record — what
# was researched, what it was weighed against, what was REJECTED and why, and
# where it ranks. The shape is MADR's. It exists because an idea had nothing
# structured to be compared against: a fitted array went into a look system
# already known to be a pile of fitted constants with nothing at decision time
# saying so, and one defect was diagnosed three times in one evening with every
# rejected diagnosis thrown away. Both directions, and the record has to be
# ABOUT the item that claims it — a backfill filed two of eleven under each
# other's keys while every existence check stayed green.
also=tools/decisions-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: every knob declares whether it acts on the whole
# photograph or on a selection, and a whole-frame one says WHY. The owner's rule
# said twice — do not apply to the photo as a whole when it should be surgical,
# especially when the thing is already easy to separate — and it was prose both
# times. What that cost, measured 2026-09-17: three whole-frame constants shipped
# in one day, each through eleven gates and five browser walks, because not one
# of them asks whether the knob should have been aimed. Meanwhile src/sky.ts has
# exported buildSkyMask for months and no look or correction uses it. A DECLARED
# LIST rather than a pattern, because no parser tells a legitimately global knob
# from a lazily global one — exposure IS global, a sky's colour noise is not. A
# reason beginning OWED is an admission and the list is the standing record of
# them.
also=tools/scope-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: every creative field a look writes is in the tile's
# stamp. The stamp is load-bearing twice over — restripForGrade compares it to
# find tiles showing a picture the app would no longer make, and previewKey
# hashes it into the key a rendered preview is STORED under, on the device,
# across releases. Decision 019 moved a look's foliage from 2.0 to 1.6 and its
# sky saturation from 1.0 to 1.8, and gradeStamp() returned the same string as
# before: neither field was in the list, nor were nine others. Nothing could
# see it — preview-version-check hashes main.ts through two named functions on
# purpose, so a look's numbers are outside its reach by design. Both lists are
# read out of the source; an exclusion must say why the field belongs to the
# photograph rather than the grade.
also=tools/stamp-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: exactly one function decides how many export workers
# start. It was worked out in two places — the gate that APPROVES a parallel
# export and the code that STARTS the workers — and on 2026-09-20 one of them
# learned that a 16-bit TIFF band weighs six bytes a pixel and the other kept
# the 4-byte default. Both sides were individually correct; what was wrong was
# that there were two sides, and since the count is monotonically larger at four
# the spawn could only ever exceed what the gate had approved. Measured on a
# tablet-class device at ~31 MP: gate 3, spawn 4, 629 MB against the 600 MB
# ceiling that exists because such a device kills the tab rather than swapping,
# and a killed tab loses the session. A test that re-derived the arithmetic
# would have passed — this refuses the SHAPE instead, so the second call site
# cannot come back.
also=tools/one-pool-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: the patch-note refusal is INSTALLED. Commit subjects are
# the in-app patch notes, and five of them reached the ⓘ dialog saying nothing
# about whether each was a defect put right or something new — one ended "and
# says what it cost", which is about the diagnostic report and means nothing in
# a list of changes. The refusal itself has to be a `commit-msg` hook, because
# the subject does not exist yet when pre-commit runs; and .git/hooks is empty
# in every fresh container, so the gate's absence looks exactly like it passing.
# This is the half that can run here: the hook is there and matches the tracked
# copy. It reads "user-facing" out of vite.config.ts rather than restating it.
also=tools/patch-note-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: the export pool is sized for what a band ACTUALLY weighs.
# one-pool-check beside this refuses a SECOND caller of workerCount, which is
# the shape the 2026-09-20 defect took. It says nothing about whether the one
# remaining decider is told the truth: if bytesPerPixel ever returns 4 for a
# format whose band is a Uint16Array, every call site agrees with every other
# and all of them are wrong together, and the 600 MB tablet ceiling is breached
# in silence again by one function instead of two. So this reads the real
# weight out of the band's own allocation in src/export.ts and requires the
# pool the app would start to equal the pool those bytes buy — two routes to
# one number, neither of them a re-derivation of the memory model.
also=tools/pool-budget-check.mjs

# Run on every commit: the shadow measurement returns unity on a frame that has
# nothing to correct. IR-SCIENCE 9j built the obvious fix for an infrared
# shadow's red cast — find the hue, add 180 degrees, tint the shadows — watched
# it work beautifully on one frame, and then measured 858,273 grey pixels driven
# from saturation 0.005 to 0.989 on a frame whose shadows were a roof rather
# than a tree. The per-photograph measurement that replaces it is only safe
# because it cannot invent a cast, and that is an arithmetic property: the gain
# compares chromaticity rather than colour, carries unit luma so it cannot move
# brightness, and comes back at exact unity on every frame it cannot measure.
# All three are cheap to assert and none of them is visible in a rendering, so
# they are asserted here rather than looked for later. It caught two real
# defects on its first run.
also=tools/shadow-cast-check.mjs
